DRAFT — AWAITING THREE-LENS REVIEW

# The V3 Quality Charter — the bar V3 is held to

Spec-pack artifact 4 of 4 (DR-047, superseding DR-001's artifact-4 wording) · Mission REQ-V3-GREENFIELD-R1
Authoring ticket: `../wayfinder/issues/15-race-victory-criteria.md` (transformed by DR-047)
Authority: `../wayfinder/decisions-ledger.md` — where this charter and a DR disagree, the DR wins.
Status: draft; not accepted until three lenses review, the orchestrator merges, and V accepts (DR-006).

---

## 0. Why this document exists, and how to read it

**The race is retired.** V ruled: *"we are not interested in competing with V2 — it's not live,
it's a prototype"* (DR-047). No formal race, no frozen victory criteria, no control-arm ceremony, no
matched-cost law, no V2 pin. V2 survives as a **reference** — where good designs and diagnosed
diseases were found (DR-033) — and humans may compare V2 and V3 outputs **informally, at will**.
That comparison is never a gate.

**What replaces it is an absolute bar:** five standing clauses, listed by V in DR-047 and expanded
below into acceptance criteria. A stranger should be able to read any criterion and restate what
passing and failing look like; a criterion that cannot be restated is a bug in the criterion.

**Two kinds of line.** An **ACCEPTANCE ITEM** (`A…`, `S…`) is a check that must pass before V accepts
V3; each names the ruling it comes from, and items bind once V accepts this charter, not before. A
**DRAFT — V RULE** (`VR-…`) is a proposal the author will **not** adopt without a V ruling, because
it fixes a number, a threshold or a vocabulary — nothing in section 7 is law. This charter
cross-references the other spec-pack artifacts rather than restating them, and lists contradictions
(section 8) without resolving any.

## 1. Clause 1 — the best dialectical engine to date, judged by V on outputs

**The clause.** V3 aims to be the best dialectical reasoning engine built to date. The judge is
**V**, and the evidence is **outputs** — real answers to real questions, with their receipts.

- **A1.1 — The judgment reads outputs, not reports.** An answer is judgeable only when served with
  its per-node provenance and execution digest (DR-027; manifest §9.2g); an agent's summary of how
  well the run went is not evidence.
- **A1.2 — A recorded judging set.** Before acceptance, V is shown served answers to a written
  question set spanning the abstention matrix's classes and risk tiers (DR-011, DR-012), recorded so
  the same questions can be re-served after any change. Its contents are V's to choose; the
  requirement is that it exists in writing.
- **A1.3 — No proxy metric may stand in for V's judgment** (DR-039, anti-theater). Numbers may
  inform V; none may be declared "the bar", and none may be minted to manufacture confidence.
- **A1.4 — V2 comparison is informal and optional** (DR-033, DR-047). V may run the same question
  through V2 and compare by eye at will. **No V3 test, gate or acceptance item compares a V3 output
  to a V2 artifact, at any level** (manifest §12.3, "Not a use").
- **A1.5 — What failure looks like.** V reads the outputs and cannot tell what the engine did, how
  sure it is, or what would change its mind. That is a charter failure, not a taste dispute.

## 2. Clause 2 — human-oriented answers: the whole-graph stranger law is the acceptance test

**The clause.** The answer is for a human who knows nothing about the machinery. V's standing test
(`../wayfinder/GLOSSARY.md`): could the person who asked read **all nodes and the verdict** and
correctly tell someone else what the answer is, how sure we are, what would change our mind, and
what they should now do differently? DR-018 amended R9 to the **whole-graph** form — every node
*and* the verdict, individually restatable — and DR-031 ratified it as spec law.

- **A2.1 — Whole-graph coverage, not a readable summary over an unreadable graph.** Every node
  carries its own plain-language restatement, written for a stranger rather than for the debate
  (manifest §6.2, items 1–13).
- **A2.2 — Coverage is enforced at the rate DR-019 knob 1 sets:** load-bearing nodes exhaustively,
  **always**; non-load-bearing sampled at a rate **derived from the asker's own run parameters**
  (depth, agent count), **auto-ratcheting up on failures**. The rate in force is recorded, so a run
  that passed cheaply is not mistaken for one that passed hard.
- **A2.3 — The test is a serving gate, not a report.** A node carries a stranger-test status, and a
  failing load-bearing node **blocks serving** (manifest §6.2 item 4). A test that can only warn is
  indicted under clause 4's G3.
- **A2.4 — Four things or it fails.** A restatement omitting what the claim is, how sure, what would
  change it, or what to do differently, fails. **No bare numbers in the top layer** — "scored 0.62
  with 0.31 uncertainty" is the shape the law forbids (manifest §6.2 item 5).
- **A2.5 — The served text is machine-checked against the computed facts.** Under serve composition
  (the Theme 6 ruling on the DR-046 row, cited elsewhere as DR-044) one model writes the text from a
  fact bundle, a second judges text↔facts conformance, and the **machine enforces the verdict** —
  mismatch means recompose or defect flag. Acceptance requires zero unenforced mismatches.
- **A2.6 — The riders carry.** Q27's uncovered-scope statement ("what the split does not cover")
  serves in plain language; Q28's "say it back cold" rider applies to the verdict (DR-031).
- **A2.7 — Honesty surfaces are part of being human-oriented, not decoration:** typed abstention
  badges, ways-of-knowing, defeaters as visible first-class attacks, STALE/UNDER-REVIEW badges, value
  markers with reversal points, SKIPPED-BY-BUDGET and fallback labels, UNDER-EXPLORED, and
  investigate-deeper all reach the reader (DR-048; DR-045; DR-015; DR-017).

## 3. Clause 3 — a clean, maintainable codebase

**The clause.** The code a person opens in a year must be readable, and each behavior must live in
exactly one place. "Maintainable" is made checkable below; it is not a mood.

- **A3.1 — One of each.** One graph, one scoring arithmetic, one serve layer (DR-030 J1–J3); two
  implementations of one behavior is a defect. The manifest's "one rule, not two" cases are instances
  — which node states enter the scored graph and the debug facet (§6.4, §9.3).
- **A3.2 — Invariants are enforced once, at write time.** Node type, lifecycle vocabulary, non-blank
  claim, path/depth consistency and acyclicity are rejected at write, not by convention and not only
  at compute time (manifest §6.2). A rule enforced only at a boundary is one the rest of the system
  does not have.
- **A3.3 — No silent failure.** Every caught failure is typed **and written to the ledger** (DR-027);
  a swallowed exception is a defect, not a style preference.
- **A3.4 — Every house rule is a standing gate.** All eight Proposal-B rules carry as required
  behaviors (DR-029), each expressed as a test (manifest §11, §12.2 layer 3). A house rule that
  cannot be expressed as a gate is one V3 cannot prove it kept.
- **A3.5 — Constants live in the ratified register, never as source literals** (DR-023). Selecting
  behavior by a source literal is D2's shape, forbidden by the defect register.
- **A3.6 — The maintenance test (clause 2's law, pointed at the code).** An engineer new to the repo
  can name, for any served behavior, the single place where it is decided. If the honest answer is
  "two places", A3.1 has already failed.

## 4. Clause 4 — NO ORPHANED MODULES

**The clause.** *Everything shipped is reachable and called.* Dead code that eats tokens and
processing is indicted at code level — the code-level echo of the disease this mission diagnosed
twice in V2: a check that exists, costs money, and cannot fire.

**The diseases, by reference** (evidence lives in the cited documents; not duplicated here):
**D5**, calibration whose learning path is unreachable, so every judge weighs a constant 1.0 while
the system presents itself as a weighting system (DR-026; manifest §10.5); **the dead check**, a
cross-judge disagreement gate un-fireable against its own data — largest observed spread 0.11
against a 0.35 threshold (DR-032; manifest §5.2i); and **never-called surfaces** — the ten V2 UI
surfaces nobody calls plus the dual-transport seam, ruled dead by DR-048, and the unused
self-consistency estimator with no caller anywhere (manifest §3.1).

**Definition a stranger can apply.** A shipped unit — module, function, endpoint, table, migration,
config flag, prompt — is **live** if (a) it is reachable from a declared entry point and (b) it is
actually called on a real run. Anything else is an orphan and does not ship.

### The five enforcement gates

- **G1 — Reachability audit (static, blocking).** Every shipped unit is reachable from a **named**
  entry point, and the audit publishes the entry-point list it walked — an unnamed entry point is how
  orphans hide. Unreachable ⇒ delete, or carry an exemption (VR-4).
- **G2 — Call-coverage audit (dynamic, blocking).** The acceptance run marks every shipped unit
  called at least once; the **never-called list** ships with the release, each entry deleted or
  exempted. A silent entry fails the release.
- **G3 — The un-fireable-path test (D5's lesson; DR-032's "demonstrably fire").** Every gate,
  threshold, branch or refusal path that can block, downgrade, flag, route or suppress must be
  **demonstrated firing on real data before it ships**. A gate that cannot fire is dead code wearing
  a gate's clothes. G3 binds at minimum: the disagreement flag (VR-1), the citation hard-kill gate
  and the coverage gate (DR-020 knobs 7–8), the stranger-test serving block (A2.3), the evidence
  gate, the abstention refusals (DR-010–012), the cold-start exit (DR-046), and the cycle policy once
  OD-23 rules.
- **G4 — Configuration-reachability test.** No configuration branch may be permanently unreachable.
  Every branch of every register flag is exercised by a test constructing a configuration a
  **production caller can actually produce** — the precise failure D5 records.
- **G5 — Dead-cost indictment.** A unit spending tokens or compute whose output no served surface,
  no ledger row and no downstream decision consumes is an orphan **even if reachable and called** —
  V2's computed-then-discarded "why it matters" sentence is the shape (manifest §6.2 item 6).

### Acceptance items

- **A4.1** — G1, G2, G4 and G5 run in continuous integration and **block** the build or release.
- **A4.2** — The never-called list ships with every release: empty, or itemized with a reason.
- **A4.3** — Exemptions are dated, named, V-approved and expiring (VR-4); expired ones fail the build.
- **A4.4** — Every gate G3 binds has a **recorded firing demonstration** in the acceptance bundle,
  naming the real data it fired on.
- **A4.5** — The acceptance bundle names the entry points G1 walked and the run G2 measured.

## 5. Clause 5 — research-upgradeable

**The clause.** Validated findings land at each step **without re-architecture** — meaning no change
to call sites, wire shapes or stage boundaries: a new implementation behind an existing interface, or
a new value in the register.

**The seams that already make this true** (ruled; listed so they are not re-litigated):

- **Swappable semantics** behind a strategy interface (H4, DR-029), strengthened by M2/Q47: both
  combination operators computable on demand, so the rival reading serves beside the deciding one.
- **Always-evolving weights and capabilities** (DR-046): updated every factually-settled round, with
  an exploration share so no model holds a monopoly, and a Postgres model ledger (DR-024) that
  next-session assignment reads.
- **Provisional values are register rows, never literals** (DR-023), on DR-012's pattern:
  provisional-by-design, recalibrated by a Stage-11 job **with V sign-off**. DR-046's probation
  figures (~50 rounds, n≥30, ~293) and DR-016's retirement N are of this kind.
- **The value overlay is detachable** and provably touches no strength (DR-017; manifest §12.2).
- **One graph substrate** (DR-030 J2) — a new stage reads what exists rather than minting a fourth
  model of the same thing.

### Acceptance items

- **A5.1 — The upgrade drill, run once before acceptance.** Land three changes and record the diff:
  (i) a second semantics implementation swapped in by configuration; (ii) a changed weight source;
  (iii) a changed threshold — each with **no call-site edits**. A demonstration, not a promise.
- **A5.2 — Every provisional number names its owner, its recalibration trigger, and who signs off**
  (DR-012). A number with no recalibration path is a constant pretending to be a finding.
- **A5.3 — The expected cost of adopting a validated finding is a register change plus a re-run.**
  Anything larger is reported to V as an architecture event, never absorbed silently.
- **A5.4 — Learned paths must execute, not merely exist.** Cold-start exit, judge-weight learning and
  scorecard updates are all G3 subjects (DR-046, DR-026): an upgrade path that cannot run is the same
  defect as a gate that cannot fire.
- **A5.5 — Research findings land as data** — register rows, scorecards or a strategy implementation,
  not the graph shape, the ledger schema or the serve contract. Where one would, A5.3 applies.

## 6. Standing acceptance items (cross-clause)

- **S1 — The replay launch ceremony (DR-034).** V3 permanently refuses to serve a number it cannot
  recompute from its frozen records, with **no model in the replay path**, continuously self-tested.
  **At launch, one independent replay of recorded runs must pass exactly** — the proof ceremony, an
  acceptance criterion rather than a development convenience (manifest §12.2 layer 4). What
  "independent" and "exactly" mean: **VR-3**.
- **S2 — The five defect prohibitions as property tests.** D1–D5 are the register in
  `../wayfinder/GLOSSARY.md`, stated as properties of V3 itself — checkable with no V2 artifact —
  at manifest §12.2 (P-D1 … P-D5), with scenario coverage listed there. **This charter does not
  restate them.** Acceptance requires all five green over that list, plus the two literature vectors.
- **S3 — The ledger tells the truth.** Everything executed has a row, the digest is user-visible, and
  no served sentence implies a check the ledger says did not run (DR-027).

## 7. DRAFT — V RULES (proposals; V rules, nothing here is law)

### VR-1 — The disagreement-flag fire bar (DR-032's "must demonstrably fire")

DR-032 requires the flag to fire where V2 provably could not, but states no bar. Three candidates
were identified (manifest §16 item 4):

| # | Candidate bar | What launch would have to show |
|---|---|---|
| **a** | **Fires at least once** against real spreads | One real run where the flag fired, recorded |
| **b** | **Fires at a rate consistent** with observed spreads | A distribution of spreads plus an expected firing rate the observed rate matches |
| **c** | **Shown to fire both ways** before adoption — the packet's upstream rule 12 standard for gates (`../research/32-weight-derivation.md` §Q5) | A real case that fires **and** a real case that correctly does not |

**Recommendation:** adopt **(c) as the adoption bar**, with **(a) as the launch-day minimum
evidence** and **(b) as a standing SETTLE-stage monitor** once outcome data exists. Reasoning: (a)
alone is satisfiable by one hand-picked case and cannot tell a working flag from a lucky one; (b)
needs a calibrated expectation nobody has yet, so it cannot gate launch without inventing a
measurement (DR-039); (c) is the only bar proving the gate is dead in **neither** direction, and is
the standard this packet already applies to gates. **Recorded cost:** (c) needs a labelled
*should-not-fire* case, which nobody has specified. VR-1 is also a G3 subject under clause 4.

### VR-2 — Verdict band semantics (the manifest's OD-13)

OD-13 was routed to ticket 15's sitting; that sitting retired the race and rerouted the row here.
Candidate semantics, from the serve layer (manifest §9.2) and the abstention matrix (DR-010–012):

1. **Bands say what the reader should do, not where a number fell.** Proposed served states:
   **SUPPORTED**, **CONTESTED**, **UNSUPPORTED** — plus, as a separate thing entirely, a **typed
   abstention** from the battery's five kinds (not searched / searched and found nothing / measured
   and inconclusive / not runnable / a value choice). An abstention rendered as a mid-range number is
   a rule violation (`../wayfinder/GLOSSARY.md`), so it is never a band.
2. **Thresholds vary by cell, not globally** — set per **question class × risk tier**, the structure
   DR-011 ruled for abstention price, for the same reason: one number does not fit a casual lookup
   and a high-stakes causal claim. High-stakes cells demand more; casual cells less.
3. **Thresholds are register rows** (DR-023), provisional by design like DR-012's seeds, recalibrated
   only with V sign-off.
4. **Coverage gating dies with D1 and D4.** V2's rule — below 0.5 tau coverage, band
   `insufficient_scoring` while still printing the raw strength underneath — is what the defect
   register forbids. Under "no judgement, no number" there is no diluted coverage left to measure;
   the successor question is **OD-07** (at what fraction of abstained children a parent refuses to
   emit a number), and until it rules, such a parent **serves its components with no band**.
5. **Every served band names its context:** its abstention-price cell (DR-012), its way-of-knowing
   ceiling (M4, OD-12), whether the rival operator would have flipped it (Q47, DR-031), and — where
   values decided it — the conditional plus the reversal point rather than a bare band (DR-017;
   state vocabulary at OD-14).

**Recommendation:** adopt the **names and the per-cell principle** now; leave every **number** to
the register ratification (DR-023). A threshold adopted in this charter would be an invented
measurement (DR-039), and OD-13's own text says V3's boundaries are a fresh design.

### VR-3 — What "independent" and "exactly" mean in the replay ceremony (DR-034)

Candidate: **independent** = replayed by a separate execution that shares no code path with the
serving run beyond the published arithmetic, reading only the frozen records, run by a person or job
that did not produce them. **Exactly** = byte-identical served numbers, which requires the arrow
ordering to be deterministic and recorded (manifest §4.2a — float addition is not reorderable).

### VR-4 — Orphan-exemption authority (clause 4, A4.3)

Candidate: an exemption for a never-called unit is granted by **V only**, is dated, names the run
that will call it, and **expires at the next release**; an expired exemption fails the build. Open
inside this proposal: whether a shipped-but-not-yet-switched-on register flag is an orphan (§8.7).

## 8. Contradictions found (listed, never resolved)

1. **DR-047 has no row in the decisions ledger.** Its text — the race retirement and the five charter
   clauses — is appended to the **DR-048** row (ticket 16, UI binding), while the map, the intake and
   ticket 15 all cite DR-047 as a distinct ruling.
2. **Three cited-but-absent DR rows.** DR-021 (knob batch 3, cited by ticket 12) sits inside DR-030;
   DR-044 (serve composition, cited by ticket 24 and research/34) sits inside DR-046; DR-047 sits
   inside DR-048. Two rows carry two rulings each; three IDs resolve only by reading another row.
3. **The retired race is still live in the manifest.** DR-033 part 2 and manifest §12.1/§12.3 call V2
   "the race's control arm, measured end-to-end at race time"; DR-047 retires the race and leaves
   DR-025 surviving "only as reference-capture definition". Manifest revision 2 predates that.
4. **OD-13's owner no longer exists.** The manifest routes OD-13 to ticket 15's sitting, which retired
   ticket 15's premise. The manifest's readiness gate — *every open decision has a ruling* — cannot
   close through ticket 15 as written, and VR-2 here is a proposal, not that ruling.
5. **The assembly gate names a file that no longer exists.** Ticket 31 and ticket 15 §Settles name
   artifact 4 `spec-pack/race-criteria.md`; DR-047 renames it, authored here as `quality-charter.md`.
6. **The glossary still describes the retired world** — "the race" as a live requirement, and golden
   vectors as MUST-MATCH/MUST-DIFFER/UNPINNED with zero-UNPINNED as a readiness gate. Both frameworks
   were retired, by DR-047 and DR-033 respectively.
7. **Clause 4 and DR-023 pull opposite ways.** Clause 4 indicts anything shipped and not called;
   DR-023 has V ratify a fresh flag register before production, and registers naturally contain flags
   that are off. Whether an off-by-default flag is an orphan is unruled (VR-4 records, not answers).
8. **Clause 1 and DR-039 sit in tension by design.** Clause 1 makes acceptance a human judgment on
   outputs; DR-039 forbids invented measurements. Nothing forbids converting V's judgment into a
   score, and A1.3 is this charter's proposal, not a ruling.
9. **DR-032's own bar is unruled while DR-032 is FINAL.** The flag must "demonstrably fire"; the
   standard for judging that is still a proposal (VR-1) — a gate required and unmeasurable at once.
10. **Clause 4's evidence base is partly excluded from the defect register.** Dead checks are
    explicitly *not* in D1–D5 by V's steer (`GLOSSARY.md`), yet DR-047 clause 4 indicts that exact
    disease at code level — one behavior outside one register and inside a charter clause.

