## 6. Contradictions surfaced (recorded, not resolved)

Per the packet's discipline, this artifact records conflicts and leaves them to V and the
review lenses.

**C1 — Three governing rulings have no ledger row.** `DR-021` (knob batch 3: budget
skips, fallback labels, per-run ownership, measurement quota), `DR-044` (serve-
composition), and `DR-047` (race retired; the quality charter; no orphaned modules) are
cited by ID across the packet — DR-021 in `issues/12`, DR-044 in `issues/24`,
`issues/34` and `research/34`, DR-047 in `00-intake-H0.md`, `issues/15` and `map.md` —
but the decisions ledger contains **no rows with those IDs**. Their normative text is
present, appended to the **DR-030**, **DR-046** and **DR-048** rows respectively. The
ledger's own closing law reads: *"Every sub-decision of every sitting appends one DR row
here at ruling time."* This document cites the IDs as the packet uses them and notes the
ledger location; whether the ledger or the citing documents are corrected is not this
seat's call.

**C2 — Ticket 16's guard and amendments versus the ruling that closed it.** The ticket's
standing Guard says *"flex is for outputs the old contract GENUINELY can't express — the
default is adapt, not redesign"*, and its Codex F6 amendment requires a per-output
outcome vocabulary (`AS_IS | ADAPT | FLEX | DROP | NOT_UI_EXPOSED`) that is
*"EXHAUSTIVE: derived from the 71-row matrix (05) × the surface inventory (01)"*. DR-048
instead flexes **all nine** honesty surfaces, rebuilds the data layer wholesale, and
names no per-row outcome ledger. DR-048 states it *"supersedes DR-002's adapter-implied
caution; strengthens it"* — but the ticket's guard text and the F6 exhaustiveness
requirement are still live in the ticket and are not addressed by the ruling's
`supersedes` field. This artifact follows DR-048.

**C3 — The race is retired, and the packet still races.** DR-047/DR-048 retire the race
outright (*"no formal race, no frozen victory criteria, no control-arm ceremony"*), yet
`DR-025` and `DR-033` clause 2 remain in the ledger describing V2 as *"the RACE's
control arm"* measured *"at race time"*; `map.md` keeps its "Race layering" note; and the
GLOSSARY still defines **The race** as a live obligation and **Golden vectors** in terms
of MUST-MATCH, which DR-033 killed. Nothing in this artifact depends on the race, but a
reader of the packet will meet both stories.

**C4 — One marker, two knob numbers.** The ledger's knob batch 3 makes knob **9** the
budget override carrying the SKIPPED-BY-BUDGET marker and knob **10** the fallback label.
`research/33-symmetry-and-model-profiles.md` attributes `SKIPPED_BY_BUDGET` to knob 9 at
one line and to *"DR-030 knob 10"* at another. Flex row 8 uses the ledger's numbering.

**C5 — "Components and UX are kept" versus nine flexed honesty surfaces.** DR-048 keeps
the interface's components and user experience while mandating nine new honesty surfaces
that, by the substrate's own findings, cannot be rendered by the components as they
stand: *"the canvas card itself has no long-form text slot"*; `NodeScores` is a closed
8-key float record pinned on both sides; the tree carries no edges; `SynthesisPanel`
renders four provenance sections by hardcoded key and silently drops the rest. No ruling
draws the line between "the kept component" and "the flexed surface". Work items W8 and
W10 sit exactly on that line.

**C6 — The kept interface cannot receive a late badge.** Rows 4 and 9 require badges that
appear *after* an answer is finished. The kept interface closes its stream permanently at
terminality and fetches scoring exactly once per debate id with no invalidation. DR-015
mandates the badge and says nothing about transport; DR-048 says the components are kept.
Whichever way V rules row 4's push-or-pull question, one of those two commitments gives.

**C7 — Five typed abstentions, and the typed states minted since.** The battery's
abstention vocabulary is five members, and DR-044 Q55 has the machine enforce **exactly
one** of the five per open unknown. Later rulings have minted further typed non-answer
states: `UNINSTRUMENTED` (DR-045, which *blocks* a claim), `NOT_EMPIRICALLY_DECIDABLE`,
the false-assumption non-answer and the inert stop (DR-037), `UNFALSIFIED-AFTER-ROTATION`
(DR-041), `SKIPPED_BY_BUDGET` (DR-021), `AMBIGUOUS_ATTRIBUTION` (DR-044 Q54). Whether
these are abstention kinds, separate badges, or a parallel vocabulary is unruled — and
"exactly one of five" cannot absorb them as written. Flex rows 1, 3, 6 and 8 each render
some of these; this artifact keeps them in separate fields (`abstention`,
`defeater_state`, `investigation.verdict`, `execution_marks`) rather than deciding.

**C8 — An honesty surface behind an undocumented flag.** The one place in the kept
interface where an unknown typed value degrades gracefully — `VerdictBanner`'s raw-band
fallback — is gated on `NEXT_PUBLIC_VERDICT_FIRST_UI === "true"`, whose default the
substrate could not find in `deploy/`, `web/package.json`, `next.config.mjs`, or any
launchd plist: *"the verdict banner may be dark in the current deployment"*. Rows 1 and 4
put content in or beside that banner. Whether V3 keeps the flag, defaults it on, or
deletes it is unruled.

**C9 — Scope boundary on the death list.** DR-048 says the ten never-called surfaces
"die", and charter clause 4 indicts unreachable code. Three of the ten are worker-plane
or health surfaces that the interface never owned and the system needs; three others name
capabilities that return inside flexed surfaces. Section 3 records a reading
(NOT-UI-PLANE / ABSORBED / DEAD) and stamps it `DRAFT—V RULES` rather than assuming the
literal one.

---

## 7. Acceptance criteria for this artifact

This document is done when a builder can answer all of the following without asking V:

1. For each of the 14 consumed surfaces: what replaces it, and what the interface must
   rebuild. **(Section 2 — 14 of 14.)**
2. Which surfaces do not enter V3's interface contract, and on what evidence.
   **(Section 3 — 11 entries.)**
3. For each of the nine honesty surfaces: what the reader sees, which ruling requires it,
   why today's contract cannot carry it, and the minimal shape that can.
   **(Section 4 — 9 rows.)**
4. What the interface work is, and in what order. **(Section 5 — 19 items.)**
5. What is still V's to decide. **(Section 4's 27 `DRAFT—V RULES` cells, plus
   section 6.)**

The stranger test applies to this document as it applies to the engine (R9, DR-018): a
reader with no project history should be able to say back what V3 will show a person that
V2 could not, and what has to be built for that to be true.

