### 23.B Cross-run memory — 24 rows (owner: artifact 1, §17)

| ID | The decision, in one line |
|---|---|
| `OD-M-01` | Does the memory key **drop** `as_of` and `policy_version` from identity, carrying them as link attributes, so a question asked under a later cutoff can still match? |
| `OD-M-02` | Which tiers may **auto-link** — exact + same-binding only, exact only, all four with a visible label, or none (every link asker-confirmed)? |
| `OD-M-03` | Where does the partial-binding / term-overlap middle band go — served as related-only, the asker's steering menu, or both by risk tier? |
| `OD-M-04` | Is embedding similarity permitted **at all**, as a candidate generator only, or barred entirely so the tiers are database predicates and nothing else? |
| `OD-M-05` | How does the alias/normalization table get its rows — deployment-supplied vocabulary, model-proposed auto-applied, or model-proposed as candidates written only when a link is confirmed? |
| `OD-M-06` | Is `NULL` ever agreement? A binding field empty on both runs — never counts and is reported as *not compared*, or counts as agreement? |
| `OD-M-07` | Link or merge — a typed directed edge with per-run identity preserved forever and no transitive closure, or merged identity on an exact match? |
| `OD-M-08` | Which error is declared **dominant** — false merge, missed reunion, or no declared asymmetry? |
| `OD-M-09` | How deep does the pull go — link + settlement facts + open triggers; through graph revival bound to the narrow tiers; link only; or through prior prose? |
| `OD-M-10` | Is the **prior served prose** barred from every prompt and display-only? |
| `OD-M-11` | Pulled looked-up claims whose locators no longer resolve — re-verify at pull, verify lazily for load-bearing nodes only, or downgrade all pulled evidence and re-promote on verification? |
| `OD-M-12` | What is the declared **pull cap**, and is it a flat number or derived from the asker's depth parameters? |
| `OD-M-13` | **Push as well as pull** — does a contradicts-prior link fire a DR-015 wake-up on the prior answer, or do prior answers wake only on their own watched triggers? |
| `OD-M-14` | Is a prior **verdict** ever admissible as evidence for its own claim — never (only the resolver's outcome and re-verifiable sources), or admissible downgraded? |
| `OD-M-15` | Does a carried posterior become the new run's prior, with `prior_basis` typed and no `DEFAULT`/`ASSUMED` member — and does the pull land **after** Q5 so the stated prior stays genuine? |
| `OD-M-16` | Does memory shape **AIM** — prior open triggers, unresolved falsifiers and error attribution seeding this run's queries and ignorance ledger — or is it serve-only? |
| `OD-M-17` | Which tier revives an **archived** graph under DR-016's auto-revive clause — the two narrow tiers only, or any candidate? |
| `OD-M-18` | **Blind-first?** Prior recorded before any pull (no extra compute); full blind-then-reveal on affected nodes (roughly double compute there, buying a measured "what memory changed" delta); high-stakes only; or none? |
| `OD-M-19` | Must a **found-but-unlinked** candidate be served, so the asker can distinguish "no history" from "history judged too weak"? |
| `OD-M-20` | **Whose memory?** Per-asker; per-deployment (one asker's question may pull another's prior session); or per-deployment for class-level facts and per-asker for question-level pulls? |
| `OD-M-21` | Where does the **interpreting model** sit — serve composition only (it narrates, never changes the run); a typed reconciliation call at LOCK/AIM that does change the run, bound to the narrow tiers and artifact-traceable adjustments; or both? |
| `OD-M-22` | Does a match ever **reduce work** — never (a link changes inputs and disclosure, never the activation set), or may named rows be satisfied from the prior run? |
| `OD-M-23` | Scorecard `n` under clustering — count distinct question-clusters, report both and cluster-adjust the interval, or ignore and record the bias in the caveats? |
| `OD-M-24` | Are **both** cold-start acceptance tests required — the inertness proof and the firing proof — or one, or neither? |

